<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Europe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> is the eastern part of the European continent, including the plains between the Baltic, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the Urals. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> dominated much of its south-eastern steppe and trade routes, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> conquered the Rus' principalities and raided deep into the region in the thirteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eastern-europe.jpg" alt="Eastern Europe" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Przemek Pietrak · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Europe_from_space.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> is a subregion of the European continent, and the term is largely ambiguous. Its eastern boundary is conventionally drawn along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Ural Mountains</a>, while its western boundary is defined in many ways and has shifted over time. Much of the region lies on the East European Plain, whose highest point, in the Valdai Hills northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, reaches only 343 metres. It is drained by long rivers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, the longest in Europe, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The historian Larry Wolff has argued that the idea of Eastern Europe was created in the 18th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> thinkers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region's history was shaped by the migrations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> in the 6th and 7th centuries and by the Christianization of its peoples from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. A treaty of 681 recognising the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> marks the beginning of the Bulgarian state. In 882 Oleg made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, the first East Slavic state, and in 964–965 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> capitals. The Mongol invasions, and later the Ottoman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> and Russian empires and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, shaped its political map.</p><p>In 1526 Suleiman destroyed the Hungarian army at Mohács, and the Ottoman advance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> ended at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683. In June 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> with about 600,000 troops, of whom fewer than 30,000 returned. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> new or restored states emerged, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> of 23 August 1939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> divided the region into spheres of influence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>From 1945 to 1989 most of the region was under communist rule, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> the term Eastern Europe was used almost as a synonym for the Soviet-dominated Eastern Bloc; historians generally regard definitions based on that usage as outdated. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">Revolutions of 1989</a> many countries joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> entered the Union on 1 May 2004, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on 1 January 2007, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> on 1 July 2013. The region's traditions include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> icon painting, the literature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Alexander Pushkin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-mickiewicz">Adam Mickiewicz</a>, and the music of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Frédéric Chopin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eastern Europe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The khans pursued their own policy in <b>Eastern Europe</b>: Islam III Giray allied with the Cossack hetman Bohdan Khmelnytsky in 1648, contributing to victories at Zhovti Vody and Korsun before abandoning him at Berestechko in 1651, and Mehmed IV Giray helped Hetman Ivan Vyhovsky defeat a Russian army…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the surrender of 8 May 1945 the Allies abolished the German state in law and divided Berlin and the remaining territory into four occupation zones; around 12 million ethnic Germans had been expelled from across <b>Eastern Europe</b>, and Germany had lost roughly a quarter of its pre-war territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the late seventh century they controlled the trade corridors linking <b>Eastern Europe</b>, Central Asia and the Near East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After independence the estates of the Muslim aristocracy were broken up, so that, unusually for <b>Eastern Europe</b>, the peasantry owned its land, though through the zadruga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Novgorod is the most important example of a republican city-state in medieval <b>Eastern Europe</b>, and its history has often been used, by nineteenth-century liberals and Decembrists as well as later historians, to show that Russian history contained a tradition of self-government before Muscovite autocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Omeljan Pritsak their homeland lay between the Aral Sea and the middle course of the Syr Darya, on trade routes linking Central Asia with <b>Eastern Europe</b>, and they descended from the Kangars, listed among the subject peoples of the Eastern Turkic Khaganate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
